<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  EXPERIMENTAL, AND A PROBE, NOT A PRODUCT. This definition exists to
  answer one question on a real AWIPS build: can a D2D derived parameter
  read a field at a previous forecast hour? It is Phase 0 of the warm
  seclusion wind project - see docs/TIME_ACCESS.md, which explains the
  mechanism, what this probe settles, and how to read its result.

  Delete this file once the answer is recorded in README.md's validation
  log. It is not meant to ship to forecasters and it is deliberately
  absent from cpsFields.xml and cpsStyleRules.xml, so it will appear in
  the Product Browser but not in the Volume Browser's CPS menu group.

  What it computes: the 12 hour change in 1000 hPa geopotential height,
  Z(F) - Z(F-12h), within the same model run. Over a deepening low this
  must be NEGATIVE. That sign is the whole point of the probe - see
  TIME_ACCESS.md section 5, which also covers what all-zeros and
  all-blank mean.

  Why no new Python: this uses the baseline `Difference` function, so
  nothing in this package is between the framework and the result. If
  the field is wrong, the framework is wrong, not our code.

  Expect it to be BLANK before forecast hour 12. There is no frame 12
  hours earlier in the same run. TIME_ACCESS.md section 6.

  The two attributes doing the work:
    - ftime="true" on the Method tells the framework to honor timeShift
      on the Field tags, taking the shifted field from the SAME model
      run at a different forecast time. (dtime="true" would instead
      take it from any model run with data at that offset, silently
      mixing cycles - never use it for a tendency.)
    - timeShift="-43200" is 12 hours in seconds. It must be written in
      seconds, not as a timestep count: a magnitude below 60 is read as
      a multiple of the model's intrinsic timestep, and output spacing
      coarsens at longer leads, so the field would change meaning
      partway across the forecast.

  VERIFY against an existing definition in
  /awips2/edex/data/utility/common_static/base/derivedParameters/definitions/
  that on your AWIPS version:
    - `ftime` and `timeShift` are spelled as below. They are documented
      in the NWS AWIPS-2 Application Focal Point derived parameter
      course, Appendix A, but no field in this package has used them
      before, so this is their first exercise on this site.
    - a NEGATIVE timeShift means the PAST. The reference calls it a
      "time offset" without stating a direction. If this field comes out
      positive over a deepening low, the convention is reversed and
      every timeShift in the package flips sign.
    - the baseline `Difference` function takes its arguments in the
      order (minuend, subtrahend), i.e. returns the first Field minus
      the second. Check the function itself under
      /awips2/edex/data/utility/common_static/base/derivedParameters/functions/.
      If it subtracts the other way, swap the two Field elements below
      rather than changing the timeShift.
    - the geopotential height field's abbreviation is "GH" as used
      below, and fixed-level Field references use the "1000MB" spelling
      - both already confirmed on this site by cps_HVTL.xml and
      cps_HCPSidx.xml.
    - unit="" (empty) is accepted, as it is for this package's other
      fields.
-->
<DerivedParameter abbreviation="HdZ12" name="PROBE 1000 hPa 12 h height change (m)" unit="">
    <Method name="Difference" levels="Surface" ftime="true">
        <!-- Z at the requested forecast hour. No timeShift: with
             ftime, only the fields that ask to be shifted are shifted.
             If this one moves too, the result is a field of zeros. -->
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="1000MB"/>
        <!-- Z 12 hours earlier in the same run. -43200 s = 12 h. -->
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="1000MB" timeShift="-43200"/>
    </Method>
</DerivedParameter>
